@PFP/1 task=CT-FINITE-DISTINGUISHABILITY-REPORT-FIBERS stage=P0 attempt=a2-1 in=f9a98139 guided=yes
@TARGET [PROVED_LEAN] Formalized finite statement. For a finite set X and a report map q from X to R, two attributes A and B of X (nonemptiness is not needed) are separated by a deterministic task, meaning a function f from R to {0,1} with f(q(a))=0 on A and f(q(b))=1 on B, if and only if q(A) and q(B) are disjoint. Checked in Lean 4 over Finset for arbitrary types X and R with decidable equality on R: both directions and the biconditional; when q(A) and q(B) are disjoint the indicator of B on the union of A and B is constant on the fibers of q (HistoryReport.FiberConstant, the form PG-HISTORY-REPORT-CRITERION takes); and an exhaustive bitmask decision check over all (q, A, B) with nonempty attributes for 1<=|X|<=4 and 1<=|R|<=3. This matches the constructor-theoretic notion of distinguishable attributes only on its deterministic finite fragment: accuracy, repeatability and constructor existence are not modelled.
@BOTTLENECK Checked in Lean 4 on Finset for all finite carriers and by the exhaustive check for |X|<=4, |R|<=3. Whether the finite condition deserves the name "distinguishable attributes" in the source's sense depends on the imported definition, which this repository does not adopt.
